10:32:54 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Google Earth is not built from one kind of image source. The sharp imagery and 3D buildings usually come from a mix of: - **Aircraft imagery:** Much of the detailed city imagery is taken from planes flying a few thousand meters above the ground, not satellites hundreds of kilometers up. Aircraft can capture images at resolutions of a few centimeters per pixel—enough to see cars, roof details, road markings, and often window patterns. - **Satellite imagery:** Satellites provide broad coverage, especially outside cities. Commercial satellites can be quite sharp, but their imagery is generally less detailed than specialized aerial surveys. Publicly available satellite imagery is often around 30–50 cm per pixel, while aerial imagery can be far finer. - **Multiple angled photos:** For “3D” cities, Google or its providers photograph an area from many directions—straight down and at angles. Software compares matching features across photos to calculate depth, a technique called **photogrammetry**. - **3D reconstruction:** The software turns those overlapping images into textured 3D meshes. It does not necessarily “know” a building has exactly 24 windows; it sees repeated visual patterns in the photos and maps them onto the building surface. At close zoom, some windows and edges may look warped, duplicated, blurry, or missing because they are reconstructed from images. - **Existing map/building data:** Some buildings may use architectural models, government GIS data, business-provided models, or manually created simplified shapes. Landmark buildings are more likely to receive special treatment. So a skyscraper may appear accurate down to its windows because it was photographed from a relatively low-flying aircraft from several angles, then reconstructed and textured—not because a normal satellite camera zoomed in from space like a telescope. Also, the imagery is often not all from the same date: the ground photo, building texture, roads, and 3D model may have been collected or updated at different times.
