10:33:24 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A satellite *can* use a powerful telescope, and some military satellites likely do have extremely capable optics. But “zoom” alone does not create detail. A long lens mainly makes a small part of the scene fill more pixels; to actually resolve finer features, the camera needs enough **optical resolution**. The problem is distance: - An aerial survey plane might photograph a city from roughly **1–5 km up**. - An imaging satellite is often around **500–700 km up**. That is hundreds of times farther away. To distinguish the same tiny detail from 500 km away, the satellite needs a much larger telescope aperture—roughly proportionally larger, depending on conditions. For example, if a plane can clearly image a 10 cm-wide feature with a relatively manageable camera, a satellite needs an enormous, extremely precise telescope, plus: - a very stable platform moving around **7–8 km per second** - cameras that gather enough light during a very short exposure - correction for haze and turbulence in the atmosphere - huge data storage and downlink capacity - narrow coverage: high detail means photographing a much smaller patch of ground at a time And it would be vastly more expensive to launch and operate. There are also restrictions. In the US, commercial satellite imagery has historically been limited to around **30 cm per pixel** resolution. That is good enough for cars, road lanes, roof equipment, and broad building features, but not reliably individual windows. Aerial mapping imagery can be around **5–15 cm per pixel** or better, which is why cities in Google Earth can look remarkably detailed. So the key distinction is: > A zoom lens can make an image bigger, but it cannot recover detail that the telescope, sensor, distance, atmosphere, and motion prevented it from capturing. Google Earth makes the transition look like one camera zooming from orbit to street level, but at the sharpest levels it is generally swapping to imagery gathered much closer to Earth—often from aircraft.
