10:33:41 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Spy satellites may be sharper than commercial imagery, but “can read license plates from space” is mostly an unverified popular claim—not a confirmed public capability. Military satellite performance is classified, so nobody outside those programs can say exactly what they can do. They likely can identify vehicles, equipment, activity, and possibly capture extremely detailed images under ideal conditions. But reliably **reading the characters on a standard license plate** from a typical low-Earth orbit satellite would require resolving features only a few centimeters across, through the atmosphere, from hundreds of kilometers away, while moving about 7.5 km/s. That is a much harder task than simply seeing the vehicle or even seeing that it has a plate. There is a real physical limit involved. A satellite at roughly 500 km altitude needs a very large, exceptionally stable telescope to resolve tiny ground features. For perspective, an ideal 2.4 m telescope—Hubble-sized—has a diffraction-limited ground resolution on the order of roughly 10–15 cm from that height at visible wavelengths, before real-world atmospheric blur, motion, lighting, and sensor limitations. Reading plate characters would generally need substantially finer usable detail than that. So the sensible version is: - **Commercial satellites:** can be very sharp, commonly enough for cars, roofs, roads, construction, some larger objects—but not dependable license-plate reading. - **Military satellites:** probably have better sensors and processing than commercial ones, but exact capability is unknown. - **“Reads license plates from space”:** possible as an intelligence-industry rumor or shorthand for very high resolution, but not something publicly demonstrated or safely assumed as routine. - **Google Earth:** its unusually sharp city imagery and window-level 3D textures are still overwhelmingly explained by aircraft-based aerial photography and photogrammetry, because it is far cheaper and much more practical for mapping huge cities at that detail. So you are right that satellites are not inherently incapable of being very sharp. The correction is that Google Earth’s “crazy zoom” presentation combines satellite imagery with much closer aerial imagery; it is not evidence that one orbiting camera is resolving every window from space.
