10:33:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: “Satellite view” is mostly a **user-interface name**, meaning “a photographic overhead map,” as opposed to the drawn road/map view. It is not a claim that every pixel currently shown was taken by a satellite. Google uses a mixture of sources and generally discloses that in the imagery credits shown in Google Earth/Maps—often names such as **Maxar**, **Airbus**, local government agencies, aerial-survey companies, or Google itself. Depending on the location and zoom level, the image may be from: - a satellite, - an airplane, - a drone or vehicle in some special cases, - government aerial surveys, - or a blend/mosaic of several images. Calling the button “Aerial imagery,” “photographic map,” or “overhead imagery” would be more technically precise, but “Satellite” became the familiar consumer term long before most people cared about the source. It is similar to calling every online search a “Google search” even when results include material from many providers: it describes the experience/category, not the exact capture method. So it is fair to say the label is simplified and can be misleading. But it does not mean Google is pretending all high-zoom city imagery came from orbit; the source credits and imagery documentation distinguish satellite and aerial providers.
